<Base title="About and credits | Twofold" description="About Twofold, formerly GeneCodex, and its data sources.">
  <main class="page" id="main">
    <h1>About Twofold</h1>
    <p class="lede">Twofold compares a raw DNA file with a dated copy of SNPedia, entirely in your browser. It's a free, open source project by <a href="https://brandonsaldan.com">Brandon Saldan</a>, first released as GeneCodex in 2023.</p>
    <h2>What the results mean</h2>
    <p>Many genetic associations are uncertain, population-specific or later revised. SNPedia's magnitude is a community rating of interest, not a risk score. Good and bad are community labels. A single genotype does not diagnose a condition or tell your whole story.</p>
    <p>Consumer genotyping chips can produce false positive calls for very rare variants. Important findings should be confirmed through clinical testing before you act on them. See <a href="https://www.bmj.com/content/372/bmj.n214">Weedon and colleagues, BMJ 2021</a>.</p>
    <h2>Credits and licenses</h2>
    <p>Genetic annotations come from <a href="https://www.snpedia.com/">SNPedia</a>, a community wiki. SNPedia content and the derived reference dataset are shared under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/3.0/us/">CC BY-NC-SA 3.0 US</a>. Source entries can be read on SNPedia. The app's own source code is available under its repository license.</p>
    <p>Twofold uses self-hosted Newsreader, Schibsted Grotesk and IBM Plex Mono fonts.</p>
    <h2>Source code</h2>
    <p><a href="https://github.com/brandonsaldan/codex">View Twofold on GitHub</a>. The repository includes the file parser, the SNPedia data builder and the tests.</p>
  </main>
</Base>
